00:01:08.037 --> 00:01:09.620
CHRISTIE WILSON: So
we're here to talk

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:12.770
to you about the next
generation of CI/CD.

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:15.530
And we think that the key to
taking a huge leap forward

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:18.560
with CI/CD is Cloud
Native technology.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:21.560
So, I, for one, found myself
using this term Cloud Native

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:23.780
all the time, but I realized
that I didn't actually

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:24.750
know what it meant.

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:27.500
So this is specifically
what Cloud Native means.

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:30.680
Applications that are Cloud
Native are open source.

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:33.960
Their architecture is
microservices in containers,

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:36.890
as opposed to
monolithic apps on VMs.

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:39.650
Those containers are
dynamically orchestrated,

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:42.680
and we optimize resource
utilization and scale

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:45.430
as needed.

00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:47.557
The key to all of
this is containers.

00:01:47.557 --> 00:01:49.140
Containers have
really changed the way

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:50.970
that we distribute software.

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:53.820
So instead of building
system-specific binaries

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:55.710
and then installing
those and installing

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:57.990
a web of dependencies,
we can package up

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:00.870
the binaries with all of their
dependencies and configuration

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:03.540
that they need and
then distribute that.

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:06.790
But what do you do if you
have a lot of containers?

00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:08.800
That's where
Kubernetes comes in.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:11.770
So Kubernetes is a platform
for dynamically orchestrating

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:12.700
containers.

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:14.920
You can tell it how to
run your container, what

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:17.500
other services it needs,
what storage it needs,

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:20.060
and Kubernetes takes
care of the rest.

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:23.140
And then, in addition to that,
Kubernetes abstracts away

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:25.840
the underlying hardware,
so you get functionality,

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:28.840
like if a machine that's running
your container goes down,

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:30.370
it'll be automatically
rescheduled

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:32.340
to a machine that's up.

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:34.690
And in Google Cloud, we
have a hosted offering

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:40.220
of Kubernetes called Google
Kubernetes Engine, or GKE.

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:41.750
So this is what
Cloud Native ends up

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:43.430
looking like for most of us.

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:46.580
We use containers as our
most basic building block,

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:48.740
then we dynamically
orchestrate those containers

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:52.130
with Kubernetes and control
our resource utilization.

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:54.170
And these are the
technologies that we're using

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:55.880
to build Cloud Native CI/CD.

00:02:59.453 --> 00:03:00.370
KIM LEWANDOWSKI: Cool.

00:03:00.370 --> 00:03:02.375
So for those not
familiar with CI/CD--

00:03:02.375 --> 00:03:04.300
and it sounds like
most of you are-- it's

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:07.580
really a set of practices to
get your code built, tested,

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:08.990
and deployed.

00:03:08.990 --> 00:03:11.140
So CI pipelines are
usually kicked off

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:14.680
after a pre-submit workflow
and determine what code

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:16.540
can be merged into a branch.

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:19.480
And then there's CD, which
is what code changes you then

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:24.200
deploy to a branch either
automatically or manually.

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:26.410
And so what we've learned
is that there's not really

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:28.630
a one-size-fits-all solution.

00:03:28.630 --> 00:03:30.580
There are projects
that just want

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:32.770
something simple and just
something that works out

00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:34.750
of the box, and then
there are companies

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:37.420
with really complex
requirements and processes

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:39.340
that they must follow
as their code travels

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:40.972
from source to production.

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:47.070
So it's an exciting time for us
in this new Cloud Native world

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:48.450
for CI/CD.

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:52.340
So CI/CD systems are really
going through a transformation.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:55.440
CI systems can now be
centered around containers,

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:58.440
and we can dynamically
orchestrate those containers,

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:00.120
and using serverless
methodologies,

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:03.750
control our resource cost.

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:06.270
And with well-defined
conformant APIs,

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:08.850
we can take advantage of that
power and not be locked in.

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:14.080
But in this new world, there's
a lot of room for improvement.

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:17.620
Problems that existed
before are still true today.

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:19.660
And some are just
downright harder.

00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:22.600
If we break our services
into microservices,

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:25.930
they inherently consist of more
pieces, have more dependencies,

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:28.480
and can be difficult to manage.

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:30.600
And the terminology
is all over the place,

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:32.530
so the same words can
mean different things

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:36.060
depending on the tool.

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:37.890
And there are a lot of tools.

00:04:37.890 --> 00:04:41.060
It seems like every week,
a new tool is announced.

00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:43.110
So I can't even keep
up with all of them,

00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:45.740
and I know that our customers
are having challenges making

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:47.970
their own tooling decisions.

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:49.890
So it's great to have
this many choices

00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:52.550
but it can often lead to
fragmentation, confusion,

00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:53.450
and complexity.

00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:01.690
But when you squint at all
these continuous delivery

00:05:01.690 --> 00:05:05.300
solutions, at their core, they
all start to look the same.

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:09.040
They have a concept of source
code access, artifacts,

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:11.200
build results, et cetera.

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:12.880
But the end goal is
always the same--

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:14.920
get my code from
source to production

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:18.320
as quickly and
securely as possible.

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:20.090
So at, Google we
took a step back,

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:22.490
and after a few
brainstorming sessions,

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:25.760
we asked ourselves if we could
do the same thing to CI/CD

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:28.070
that Kubernetes did
with containers.

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:31.370
That is, could we collaborate
with industry leaders

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:34.760
in the open to define a
common set of components

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:38.870
and guidelines for CI/CD systems
to build, test, and deploy code

00:05:38.870 --> 00:05:42.210
anywhere.

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:44.820
And that is what the Tekton
project is all about.

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:48.360
Tekton is a shared set of open
source Cloud Native building

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:51.070
blocks for CI/CD systems.

00:05:51.070 --> 00:05:53.430
Even though Tekton
runs on Kubernetes,

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:56.730
the goal is to target any
platform, any language,

00:05:56.730 --> 00:06:00.180
and any framework, whether
that's GKE, on-prem,

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:03.510
multicloud, hybrid
cloud, tribrid cloud--

00:06:03.510 --> 00:06:06.500
you name it.

00:06:06.500 --> 00:06:09.670
So Tekton started as a
project within Knative.

00:06:09.670 --> 00:06:12.280
People got very excited
to be able to build images

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:13.780
on Kubernetes.

00:06:13.780 --> 00:06:15.710
But very quickly, they
wanted to do more.

00:06:15.710 --> 00:06:17.680
They wanted to run
tests on those images,

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:21.170
and they wanted to define
more complex pipelines.

00:06:21.170 --> 00:06:23.200
And as enthusiasm
grew, we decided

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:25.390
to move it out and put
it into its own GitHub

00:06:25.390 --> 00:06:26.800
org, where it became Tekton.

00:06:29.990 --> 00:06:31.700
So again, the vision
of this project

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:35.210
is CI/CD building blocks that
are composable, declarative,

00:06:35.210 --> 00:06:36.770
and reproducible.

00:06:36.770 --> 00:06:39.680
We want to make it
super easy and fast

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:42.680
to build custom extensible
layers on top of these building

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:43.460
blocks.

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:47.630
So engineers can take
an entire CI/CD pipeline

00:06:47.630 --> 00:06:49.730
and run it against their
own infrastructure,

00:06:49.730 --> 00:06:51.470
or they can take
pieces of that pipeline

00:06:51.470 --> 00:06:53.680
and run it in isolation.

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:56.240
And the more vendors
that support Tekton,

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:58.280
the more choices
users will have.

00:06:58.280 --> 00:06:59.840
And they'll be able
to plug and play

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:02.210
different pieces
from multiple vendors

00:07:02.210 --> 00:07:05.012
with the same pipeline
definition underneath.

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:10.510
So Tekton is a
collaborative effort,

00:07:10.510 --> 00:07:12.660
and we're already
working on this project

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:16.560
with companies including
Cloudbees, Red Hat, and IBM.

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:18.720
And we've made a
super big effort

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:21.330
to make it easy for new
contributors to join us.

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:26.220
And again, pipelines is
our first building block

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:27.210
for Tekton.

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:31.080
And now Christie we'll
be diving deeper.

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:32.580
CHRISTIE WILSON:
So Tekton pipelines

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:35.790
is all about Cloud Native
components for defining CI/CD

00:07:35.790 --> 00:07:36.613
pipelines.

00:07:36.613 --> 00:07:39.030
So I'm going to go into a bit
of detail about how it works

00:07:39.030 --> 00:07:41.342
and how it's implemented.

00:07:41.342 --> 00:07:42.800
So the first thing
to understand is

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:45.980
that it's implemented
using Kubernetes CRDs.

00:07:45.980 --> 00:07:49.220
So CRD stands for Custom
Resource Definition,

00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:52.460
and it's a way of extending
Kubernetes itself.

00:07:52.460 --> 00:07:54.380
So out of the box,
Kubernetes comes

00:07:54.380 --> 00:07:57.740
with resources like pods,
deployments, and services.

00:07:57.740 --> 00:08:01.050
But through CRDs, you can
define your own resources,

00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:03.140
and then you create
binaries called controllers

00:08:03.140 --> 00:08:05.580
that act on those resources.

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:09.960
So what CRDs have we added
for Tekton pipelines?

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:13.090
Our most basic building block
is something we call a step.

00:08:13.090 --> 00:08:15.760
So this is actually a
Kubernetes container spec,

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:17.480
which is an existing type.

00:08:17.480 --> 00:08:20.580
A container spec lets you
specify an image and everything

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:23.340
you need to run it, like what
environment variables to use,

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:27.530
what arguments, what
volumes, et cetera.

00:08:27.530 --> 00:08:30.350
And the first new type we
added is called a task.

00:08:30.350 --> 00:08:33.049
So a task lets
you combine steps.

00:08:33.049 --> 00:08:35.210
You define a sequence
of steps, which

00:08:35.210 --> 00:08:40.760
run in sequential order on
the same Kubernetes node.

00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:42.980
Our next new type is
called a pipeline.

00:08:42.980 --> 00:08:46.310
A pipeline lets you
combine tasks together.

00:08:46.310 --> 00:08:48.550
And you can define the order
that these tasks run in,

00:08:48.550 --> 00:08:51.030
so that can be sequentially,
it can be concurrently,

00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:53.460
or you can create
complicated graphs.

00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:57.300
The tasks aren't guaranteed
to execute on the same node,

00:08:57.300 --> 00:08:59.550
but through the pipeline,
you can take the outputs

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:01.170
from one task and
you can pass them

00:09:01.170 --> 00:09:04.770
as inputs to the next task.

00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:07.700
So being able to define
these more complex graphs

00:09:07.700 --> 00:09:09.780
will really speed
up your pipelines.

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:11.850
So for example,
in this pipeline,

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:14.270
we can get some of the
faster activities out

00:09:14.270 --> 00:09:16.880
of the way in parallel first,
like linting and running

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:18.320
unit tests.

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:20.780
Next, as we run into
some of the slower steps,

00:09:20.780 --> 00:09:22.730
like running
integration tests, we

00:09:22.730 --> 00:09:24.660
can do some of our
other slower activities,

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:27.380
like building images and
setting up a test environment

00:09:27.380 --> 00:09:28.520
for our end-to-end tests.

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:33.790
So tasks and pipelines
are types you define once

00:09:33.790 --> 00:09:35.680
and you use again and again.

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:39.100
To actually invoke those, you
use pipeline runs and task

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:42.790
runs, which are
our next new types.

00:09:42.790 --> 00:09:45.100
So these actually invoke
the pipelines and tasks.

00:09:45.100 --> 00:09:47.320
But to do that, you need
runtime information,

00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:49.960
like what image registry
should I use, what git repo

00:09:49.960 --> 00:09:51.490
should I be running against.

00:09:51.490 --> 00:09:54.280
And to do that, you use our
fifth and final type, pipeline

00:09:54.280 --> 00:09:56.910
resources.

00:09:56.910 --> 00:10:00.000
So altogether, we
added five CRDs.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:02.670
We have tasks, which
are made up of steps,

00:10:02.670 --> 00:10:05.380
we have pipelines, which
are made up of tasks,

00:10:05.380 --> 00:10:08.670
then we invoke those using
task runs and pipeline runs.

00:10:08.670 --> 00:10:11.160
And finally, we provide
runtime information

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:14.950
with pipeline resources.

00:10:14.950 --> 00:10:16.780
And decoupling this
runtime information

00:10:16.780 --> 00:10:19.270
gives us a lot of power
because, suddenly, you

00:10:19.270 --> 00:10:22.790
can take the same pipeline that
you used to push to production

00:10:22.790 --> 00:10:25.570
and you can safely run it
against your pull requests.

00:10:25.570 --> 00:10:27.610
Or you can shift
even further left

00:10:27.610 --> 00:10:29.110
and, suddenly, your
contributors can

00:10:29.110 --> 00:10:34.177
run that same pipeline against
their own infrastructure.

00:10:34.177 --> 00:10:35.760
So this is what the
architecture looks

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:37.540
like at a very high level.

00:10:37.540 --> 00:10:39.300
So users interact
with Kubernetes

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:41.250
to create pipelines
and tasks, which are

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:43.500
stored in Kubernetes itself.

00:10:43.500 --> 00:10:45.300
And then when the user
wants to run them,

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:47.700
the user creates the
runs, which are picked up

00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:50.490
by a controller, which
is managed by Kubernetes.

00:10:50.490 --> 00:10:52.650
And the controller
realizes them by creating

00:10:52.650 --> 00:10:55.050
the appropriate pods
and container instances.

00:10:59.980 --> 00:11:02.880
