that is designed to run
on Kubernetes and target

00:12:02.840 --> 00:12:03.890
Kubernetes.

00:12:03.890 --> 00:12:05.750
You can use it for
building traditional

00:12:05.750 --> 00:12:07.850
and cloud native workloads.

00:12:07.850 --> 00:12:11.510
You can create new applications
or import existing applications

00:12:11.510 --> 00:12:13.730
into Kubernetes
and take advantage

00:12:13.730 --> 00:12:16.130
of various quick
starts and build

00:12:16.130 --> 00:12:19.880
pacts that allow you to
get the initial setup

00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:22.340
of the project, et
cetera, without having

00:12:22.340 --> 00:12:23.920
to do it all by hand.

00:12:23.920 --> 00:12:26.040
It gives you fully
automated CI/CD,

00:12:26.040 --> 00:12:28.980
integrating with
GitHub via Prow,

00:12:28.980 --> 00:12:33.120
so a lot of automation and
GitOps promotions, et cetera,

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:37.020
without you actually having
to go click stuff by hand.

00:12:37.020 --> 00:12:41.730
It's got promotions, it's
got a staging, dev and prod

00:12:41.730 --> 00:12:47.290
environment integration, and
a whole lot of other magic.

00:12:47.290 --> 00:12:50.920
I'm here specifically to
talk about the part about how

00:12:50.920 --> 00:12:55.390
Jenkins X is using
Tekton Pipeline.

00:12:55.390 --> 00:13:00.490
So a user is not actually
going to necessarily know

00:13:00.490 --> 00:13:04.000
that they're using Tekton
Pipelines behind the scenes.

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:07.120
We have our own ways of defining
your pipelines in Jenkins X,

00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:09.250
either via a standard
build pack or when

00:13:09.250 --> 00:13:13.790
you define your own pipeline
using our YAML syntax.

00:13:13.790 --> 00:13:17.650
Then at runtime, when a
build gets kicked off,

00:13:17.650 --> 00:13:20.440
Jenkins X translates
that pipeline

00:13:20.440 --> 00:13:26.830
into the CRDs that are necessary
to run a Tekton Pipeline.

00:13:26.830 --> 00:13:30.580
And then Jenkins X monitors the
pipeline execution, et cetera.

00:13:30.580 --> 00:13:37.140
So that means that, like I
said, the user isn't directly

00:13:37.140 --> 00:13:38.140
interacting with Tekton.

00:13:38.140 --> 00:13:40.770
The user's interacting
with Jenkins X.

00:13:40.770 --> 00:13:44.940
That means that we can do
a lot of things on our side

00:13:44.940 --> 00:13:47.880
without having to worry about
exactly how the user is going

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:50.280
to react.

00:13:50.280 --> 00:13:53.280
So why are we using
Tekton Pipelines?

00:13:53.280 --> 00:13:55.530
Like I said, I've been
working on Jenkins pipelines

00:13:55.530 --> 00:13:59.460
as well for a while now,
and what we've come to learn

00:13:59.460 --> 00:14:01.290
is that pipeline
execution really

00:14:01.290 --> 00:14:04.290
should be standardized
and commoditized.

00:14:04.290 --> 00:14:07.770
CI/CD tools all over the place
have reinvented the wheel

00:14:07.770 --> 00:14:10.860
many times, and there's
no reason for us

00:14:10.860 --> 00:14:13.550
to keep doing that.

00:14:13.550 --> 00:14:15.920
So I'm really
excited about that.

00:14:15.920 --> 00:14:20.920
And we really like that we
can translate our syntax

00:14:20.920 --> 00:14:24.130
into what's necessary
for the pipelines

00:14:24.130 --> 00:14:25.990
to actually execute
so that we're still

00:14:25.990 --> 00:14:28.750
able to provide an opinionated
and curated experience

00:14:28.750 --> 00:14:32.960
for Jenkins X users
and pipeline authors

00:14:32.960 --> 00:14:36.080
without having to worry
about being exactly

00:14:36.080 --> 00:14:39.450
the right syntax and
verbosity, et cetera.

00:14:39.450 --> 00:14:44.720
And it gets us away from the
traditional Jenkins world

00:14:44.720 --> 00:14:47.180
of a long running
JVM, controlling

00:14:47.180 --> 00:14:52.200
all execution, which is good.

00:14:52.200 --> 00:14:54.830
But the best part
is, as Kim mentioned,

00:14:54.830 --> 00:14:56.930
how great it is to
contribute and get

00:14:56.930 --> 00:14:58.880
involved with Tekton Pipelines.

00:14:58.880 --> 00:15:02.180
I only got involved in this
at all starting in November.

00:15:02.180 --> 00:15:05.540
And we've been able to
contribute significantly

00:15:05.540 --> 00:15:09.770
to the project, help with the
figuring out direction, fixing

00:15:09.770 --> 00:15:12.020
bugs, integrating
it with Jenkins X,

00:15:12.020 --> 00:15:14.180
and get this all to the
point of being pretty much

00:15:14.180 --> 00:15:17.250
production-ready in
just a few months.

00:15:17.250 --> 00:15:18.780
And that's phenomenal.

00:15:18.780 --> 00:15:20.780
It's just been a great
experience and incredibly

00:15:20.780 --> 00:15:22.160
welcoming community.

00:15:22.160 --> 00:15:26.510
And it's been a lot of fun.

00:15:26.510 --> 00:15:30.050
I don't have an
actual demo exactly.

00:15:30.050 --> 00:15:31.130
Let me go back again.

00:15:31.130 --> 00:15:33.470
Sorry.

00:15:33.470 --> 00:15:35.000
Of course, my screen
went to sleep.

00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:35.500
Hold on.

00:15:44.570 --> 00:15:45.070
Typo.

00:15:50.360 --> 00:15:50.860
All right.

00:15:50.860 --> 00:15:52.690
So what I wanted
to show you here

00:15:52.690 --> 00:15:56.110
was just quickly how
much of a difference

00:15:56.110 --> 00:15:58.960
there is between the
syntax a user is authoring,

00:15:58.960 --> 00:16:01.150
and what Tekton
actually needs to run,

00:16:01.150 --> 00:16:03.130
and why we think
that's valuable.

00:16:03.130 --> 00:16:09.140
So this is roughly what a
obviously brain dead simple

00:16:09.140 --> 00:16:15.260
pipeline in Jenkins X would
look like, just 26 lines.

00:16:15.260 --> 00:16:20.610
And then when we
transform it, well, it's

00:16:20.610 --> 00:16:21.600
a lot more than that.

00:16:21.600 --> 00:16:25.820
But because we're able
to generate that and not

00:16:25.820 --> 00:16:27.870
require the user to
author it all by hand,

00:16:27.870 --> 00:16:34.350
we're able to inject Jenkins
X's opinions about environments,

00:16:34.350 --> 00:16:42.600
variables, about what images
should be used, and a lot more.

00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:44.700
And it's been
really great for us

00:16:44.700 --> 00:16:47.940
to be able to have the
full power of Tekton

00:16:47.940 --> 00:16:51.000
Pipelines behind the
scenes during execution

00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:54.060
without needing to make the
user have to worry about all

00:16:54.060 --> 00:16:55.800
those details all the time.

00:16:55.800 --> 00:16:58.240
So that's been really
productive for us.

00:16:58.240 --> 00:17:00.895
Thank you.

00:17:00.895 --> 00:17:04.304
[APPLAUSE]

00:17:13.902 --> 00:17:15.319
KIM LEWANDOWSKI:
So next, I'd like

00:17:15.319 --> 00:17:16.805
to introduce Chris
from TriggerMesh

00:17:16.805 --> 00:17:18.430
to talk about the
work he's been doing.

00:17:18.430 --> 00:17:19.430
CHRIS BAUMBAUER: Oh.

00:17:19.430 --> 00:17:21.190
Cool.

00:17:21.190 --> 00:17:23.210
Actually, yeah.

00:17:23.210 --> 00:17:25.010
So, thank you, Kim.

00:17:25.010 --> 00:17:25.670
Thanks, Andrew.

00:17:25.670 --> 00:17:26.760
Hi, everyone.

00:17:26.760 --> 00:17:29.300
My name's Chris Baumbauer,
developer with TriggerMesh,

00:17:29.300 --> 00:17:32.990
and also one of the
co-authors for the Tekton--

00:17:32.990 --> 00:17:35.910
sorry, for TriggerMesh's Aktion.

00:17:35.910 --> 00:17:40.130
So starting off with
TriggerMesh's Aktion,

00:17:40.130 --> 00:17:46.130
this came out as more of a
way of tying in the GitHub

00:17:46.130 --> 00:17:47.570
Actions--

00:17:47.570 --> 00:17:50.360
or, sorry, yeah-- the
GitHub Action workflow

00:17:50.360 --> 00:17:54.470
once it was announced
last October into creating

00:17:54.470 --> 00:17:58.010
a way of being able to bind
that with the Tekton Pipeline

00:17:58.010 --> 00:17:58.760
approach.

00:17:58.760 --> 00:18:02.780
The idea being that,
with that workflow,

00:18:02.780 --> 00:18:06.020
we can translate that
into the various resources

00:18:06.020 --> 00:18:09.240
that the Tekton Pipeline
now makes available,

00:18:09.240 --> 00:18:12.440
and then be able to feed that
into your Kubernetes cluster

00:18:12.440 --> 00:18:14.960
and be able to either
experiment with it

00:18:14.960 --> 00:18:19.910
or even create additional hooks
so that it can actually receive

00:18:19.910 --> 00:18:22.620
external stimuli through
something like Knative's

00:18:22.620 --> 00:18:24.050
eventing service--

00:18:24.050 --> 00:18:26.150
things such as
whether it is going

00:18:26.150 --> 00:18:29.240
to be a pull request from
something like GitHub or maybe

00:18:29.240 --> 00:18:32.450
is some other web
form being filled out

00:18:32.450 --> 00:18:36.710
that triggers that build or
that workflow in the background

00:18:36.710 --> 00:18:40.280
to provide the result, and
ultimately allowing you

00:18:40.280 --> 00:18:42.900
to run things from anywhere.

00:18:42.900 --> 00:18:48.660
So a little a bit of a mapping
exercise for the terminology

00:18:48.660 --> 00:18:51.320
[? goes. ?] With
GitHub Actions, you

00:18:51.320 --> 00:18:53.690
have the concept
of the workflow.

00:18:53.690 --> 00:18:57.030
The workflow, it would be like
the equivalent of the Tekton

00:18:57.030 --> 00:18:59.330
Pipelines global pipeline.

00:18:59.330 --> 00:19:03.230
This is where anything and
everything runs task-wise.

00:19:03.230 --> 00:19:05.780
And then as far as
the GitHub Action,

00:19:05.780 --> 00:19:09.050
that is the equivalent
of that single step

00:19:09.050 --> 00:19:13.250
or that task where it's going
to be that one container that

00:19:13.250 --> 00:19:16.370
runs that command that
produces the output.

00:19:16.370 --> 00:19:19.460
And I do call out one
of the other components

00:19:19.460 --> 00:19:22.970
within the GitHub Action,
which is called Uses.

00:19:22.970 --> 00:19:25.910
This is more of
being able to define

00:19:25.910 --> 00:19:29.390
the image that you want to run
within your Tekton Pipeline

00:19:29.390 --> 00:19:30.890
task.

00:19:30.890 --> 00:19:33.890
Whereas, with the
Tekton Pipeline tasks

00:19:33.890 --> 00:19:36.590
expect a particular
image, what we end up

00:19:36.590 --> 00:19:39.350
translating with
the GitHub Actions--

00:19:39.350 --> 00:19:43.490
or, at least, we will once
I finish my pull request--

00:19:43.490 --> 00:19:47.630
is that we'll be able to allow
the full support that GitHub

00:19:47.630 --> 00:19:49.820
has for their Actions
as far as being

00:19:49.820 --> 00:19:51.950
able to point it to
another GitHub repo

00:19:51.950 --> 00:19:56.450
or point it to some other local
directory within that repo

00:19:56.450 --> 00:20:02.910
that you have defined to be
able to build the Docker image

00:20:02.910 --> 00:20:06.590
that you can then feed into
the task to work the magic,

00:20:06.590 --> 00:20:08.180
as it were.

00:20:08.180 --> 00:20:11.900
So as far as our
little pretty picture,

00:20:11.900 --> 00:20:14.240
with the TriggerMesh
Aktion, we actually

00:20:14.240 --> 00:20:18.020
have two commands that
handle everything.

00:20:18.020 --> 00:20:20.930
We have, down at the
bottom, the create.

00:20:20.930 --> 00:20:24.710
This one creates the tasks,
creates the associated pipeline

00:20:24.710 --> 00:20:29.390
resources, and you can also
use it to create the task run

00:20:29.390 --> 00:20:34.952
or the pipeline run object to
create a one-shot invocation,

00:20:34.952 --> 00:20:36.660
usually if you want
to test something out

00:20:36.660 --> 00:20:40.190
to see if you've got your
workflow working just right

00:20:40.190 --> 00:20:43.430
or if you wanted something
else to call into this.

00:20:43.430 --> 00:20:49.370
And up above is, we also
create a Knative eventing sink,

00:20:49.370 --> 00:20:53.430
as well as the
associated transceiver,

00:20:53.430 --> 00:20:58.070
which creates a serverless
service within Knative

00:20:58.070 --> 00:21:02.540
to handle the creation and the
invocation of that task run

00:21:02.540 --> 00:21:04.590
object.

00:21:04.590 --> 00:21:09.420
So to give you a little bit of
a quick demo of what we have,

00:21:09.420 --> 00:21:10.510
let's see if I can--

00:21:10.510 --> 00:21:12.610
oh, perfect.

00:21:12.610 --> 00:21:14.190
So what we're
looking at right now

00:21:14.190 --> 00:21:19.140
is the customary
Hello World example.

00:21:19.140 --> 00:21:20.880
You'll note, up
at the top, we do

00:21:20.880 --> 00:21:25.500
have our workflow defined, this
one being more of our pipeline

00:21:25.500 --> 00:21:27.670
object.

00:21:27.670 --> 00:21:30.450
The results indicates
all of the actions that

00:21:30.450 --> 00:21:32.460
would be associated
with our workflow

00:21:32.460 --> 00:21:35.850
and the on usually indicates
the type of action that

00:21:35.850 --> 00:21:38.520
would happen within your repo.

00:21:38.520 --> 00:21:41.170
And then, right below
that one, for the action,

00:21:41.170 --> 00:21:46.380
you'll have some kind of
identifier, our naming it.

00:21:46.380 --> 00:21:49.230
The fact that we're using
centos as our base image,

00:21:49.230 --> 00:21:52.560
and, of course, we're
just going to run hello

00:21:52.560 --> 00:21:57.210
with a specification for
the environment variable.

00:21:57.210 --> 00:22:00.240
We do also see that
args does allow

00:22:00.240 --> 00:22:04.560
you to pass in either a
string or an array of strings

00:22:04.560 --> 00:22:07.590
to go in as well, which
is one of the nice things

00:22:07.590 --> 00:22:08.520
about their language.

00:22:08.520 --> 00:22:12.780
And as far as some
of our translation,

00:22:12.780 --> 00:22:15.570
we take care of that for you.

00:22:15.570 --> 00:22:18.683
So with the Aktion
command itself,

00:22:18.683 --> 00:22:20.600
as mentioned beforehand,
you have your create,

00:22:20.600 --> 00:22:22.040
you have your launch.

00:22:22.040 --> 00:22:23.540
One of the things
that we originally

00:22:23.540 --> 00:22:26.540
had started working on
is our own implementation

00:22:26.540 --> 00:22:30.840
of the parser for the GitHub
action workflow syntax,

00:22:30.840 --> 00:22:34.640
but since GitHub was kind
enough to open source

00:22:34.640 --> 00:22:37.940
it back in February so
that experimental projects

00:22:37.940 --> 00:22:41.180
such as these can
make use of it,

00:22:41.180 --> 00:22:42.930
we've started
transitioning to that one.

00:22:42.930 --> 00:22:46.340
So now it acts as more of
a sanity check on whatever

00:22:46.340 --> 00:22:48.290
workflows that you feed
in to ensure that it

00:22:48.290 --> 00:22:50.930
does what it's supposed to do.

00:22:50.930 --> 00:22:53.390
As far as the common
global arguments,

00:22:53.390 --> 00:22:56.750
we do allow for passing
in your Git repository.

00:22:56.750 --> 00:23:02.210
When it comes to creating things
such as the collection of tasks

00:23:02.210 --> 00:23:04.400
with the create
command, it is used

00:23:04.400 --> 00:23:08.990
for being able to not only
create a pipeline resource that

00:23:08.990 --> 00:23:11.270
could be referenced by
additional steps in case

00:23:11.270 --> 00:23:17.690
you wanted to add things to it,
but also in case of specifying

00:23:17.690 --> 00:23:19.880
that local directory
so that we know

00:23:19.880 --> 00:23:23.950
which repo to pull the docker
from to build the image.

00:23:23.950 --> 00:23:25.570
So now, OK.

00:23:28.330 --> 00:23:30.280
We'll feed that into
our Hello World.

00:23:30.280 --> 00:23:33.790
We just feed everything as is,
and we have our simple task

00:23:33.790 --> 00:23:34.540
object.

00:23:34.540 --> 00:23:39.420
You'll see our steps have
actually been broken down.

00:23:39.420 --> 00:23:40.545
We have our simple command.

00:23:40.545 --> 00:23:41.962
We have our
environment variables.

00:23:41.962 --> 00:23:43.460
We have our new
and improved image.

00:23:43.460 --> 00:23:45.770
And we also have
our name, which is

00:23:45.770 --> 00:23:50.900
been Kubernetified to resemble
their traditional naming

00:23:50.900 --> 00:23:52.160
scheme.

00:23:52.160 --> 00:23:58.960
And then we can pass in minus t
to provide our task run object.

00:23:58.960 --> 00:24:05.250
And then we have our
favorite, apply f.

00:24:05.250 --> 00:24:09.310
Hopefully, it'll still talk
to my Kubernetes cluster.

00:24:09.310 --> 00:24:11.500
And we have our objects
that are created.

00:24:20.030 --> 00:24:22.400
And it looks like
it just finished.

00:24:22.400 --> 00:24:24.890
So we have our true,
we have our success,

00:24:24.890 --> 00:24:27.650
and we also have a pod name
so we can go here and take

00:24:27.650 --> 00:24:29.962
a look at any output
in the case of failures

00:24:29.962 --> 00:24:31.670
or if there was
something that you wanted

00:24:31.670 --> 00:24:36.740
to grab as part of any of
the successful messages

00:24:36.740 --> 00:24:39.570
and other things
along those lines.

00:24:39.570 --> 00:24:43.450
And then, if we were
to look at launch,

00:24:43.450 --> 00:24:49.172
the one thing it does require
is that we do pass in a task.

00:24:49.172 --> 00:24:52.480
And let's see if I can--

00:24:52.480 --> 00:24:52.980
come on.

00:24:55.840 --> 00:24:59.380
This one does also require
that you specify a GitHub repo.

00:25:04.943 --> 00:25:07.320
All right.

00:25:07.320 --> 00:25:17.120
And this one, so here we have
our eventing source definition

00:25:17.120 --> 00:25:20.480
specifying going in the GitHub,
the requests for pulling

00:25:20.480 --> 00:25:26.000
in our credentials,
and also the test

00:25:26.000 --> 00:25:27.710
that it will create and fire.

00:25:27.710 --> 00:25:34.958
So that, I believe,
is pretty much it.

00:25:34.958 --> 00:25:36.000
KIM LEWANDOWSKI: Awesome.

00:25:36.000 --> 00:25:37.640
So thank you, Andrew
and Chris, again,

00:25:37.640 --> 00:25:39.690
for sharing your work with us.

00:25:39.690 --> 00:25:42.160
Like I said before, we're
not doing this alone.

00:25:42.160 --> 00:25:44.700
And Tekton is actually
part of a new foundation

00:25:44.700 --> 00:25:47.640
called the Continuous
Delivery Foundation.

00:25:47.640 --> 00:25:49.320
This is an open
foundation where we're

00:25:49.320 --> 00:25:52.890
collaborating with industry
leaders on best practices

00:25:52.890 --> 00:25:56.225
and guidelines for the next
generation of CI/CD systems.

00:26:00.240 --> 00:26:02.610
And so initial
projects of the CDF

00:26:02.610 --> 00:26:06.900
include Tekton, Jenkins,
Spinnaker, and Jenkins X. Now,

00:26:06.900 --> 00:26:09.913
you've seen Tekton integration
with Jenkins X. We're also

00:26:09.913 --> 00:26:12.330
excited that we're starting
to integrate with Spinnaker as

00:26:12.330 --> 00:26:12.830
well.

00:26:16.990 --> 00:26:19.290
And so these are the
current members of the CDF,

00:26:19.290 --> 00:26:21.720
and we're really excited to
work with them on our mission

00:26:21.720 --> 00:26:24.600
to help companies practice
continuous delivery

00:26:24.600 --> 00:26:26.880
and bring value to
their customers as fast

00:26:26.880 --> 00:26:28.065
and securely as possible.

00:26:33.525 --> 00:26:36.270
So if you want to learn
more about the CDF,

00:26:36.270 --> 00:26:38.910
please check out
cd.foundation to get

00:26:38.910 --> 00:26:41.310
involved or just kind of
watch what's going on.

00:26:45.565 --> 00:26:46.690
CHRISTIE WILSON: All right.

00:26:46.690 --> 00:26:48.300
So what's coming next?

00:26:48.300 --> 00:26:51.440
So for the CDF, we have a
co-located summit coming up

00:26:51.440 --> 00:26:54.283
in KubeCon Barcelona on May 20.

00:26:54.283 --> 00:26:55.700
And if you're
interested in what's

00:26:55.700 --> 00:26:59.000
coming down the pipeline
for Tekton Pipelines,

00:26:59.000 --> 00:27:02.120
we're currently working
on some exciting features,

00:27:02.120 --> 00:27:05.570
like conditional execution,
and event triggering, and more.

00:27:05.570 --> 00:27:07.850
And for Tekton itself,
we're looking forward

00:27:07.850 --> 00:27:09.850
to expanding the scope
and adding more projects.

00:27:09.850 --> 00:27:13.780
And we recently had a
dashboard project added.

00:27:13.780 --> 00:27:16.460
And so for takeaways, if you're
interested in contributing

00:27:16.460 --> 00:27:19.850
to Tekton or you're interested
in integrating with Tekton,

00:27:19.850 --> 00:27:21.710
please check out our
contributing guide

00:27:21.710 --> 00:27:23.060
in our GitHub repo.

00:27:23.060 --> 00:27:25.820
It has information about how
to get started developing

00:27:25.820 --> 00:27:28.400
and also how you can
join our Slack channel

00:27:28.400 --> 00:27:31.170
and our working group
meetings, et cetera.

00:27:31.170 --> 00:27:33.500
If you're an end user,
check out Jenkins X,

00:27:33.500 --> 00:27:36.530
check out TriggerMesh
Aktion, and watch this space

00:27:36.530 --> 00:27:40.377
for more exciting Tekton
integrations in the future.

00:27:40.377 --> 00:27:40.960
And that's it.

00:27:40.960 --> 00:27:43.090
Thanks so much for
listening to our talk.

00:27:43.090 --> 00:27:44.890
[APPLAUSE]

00:27:46.090 --> 00:27:49.740
